WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.473
A phone camera can read this QR code.

00:00:02.473 --> 00:00:03.907
But should you open the link?

00:00:04.147 --> 00:00:07.893
Patrick Selby built ScanSafe at
Grambling State University

00:00:07.893 --> 00:00:10.312
to show what's inside a code before you
tap.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.969
These are pictures Patrick took during
testing.

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:17.725
On the phone, ScanSafe reads a code
displayed on his laptop.

00:00:17.965 --> 00:00:21.937
The result lists three clues, including
an unencrypted connection

00:00:21.937 --> 00:00:23.982
and the word verify in the link.

00:00:23.982 --> 00:00:26.563
Together, the rules give it a score of
six.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.827
Patrick also tested links by hand.

00:00:29.827 --> 00:00:31.469
The Google address scored zero.

00:00:31.709 --> 00:00:33.739
Look closely at the second address.

00:00:33.739 --> 00:00:36.400
The last character in the brand name is
a one,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.407
where you would expect the letter L.

00:00:38.407 --> 00:00:41.757
The lookalike and the login path raised
its score to five.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.611
In the working prototype, enter a link
and select Scan.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.315
The first link scores zero.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.595
Next, the simulated camera presents a
test code.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.601
ScanSafe reads the address and scores it
eight.

00:00:57.601 --> 00:00:59.252
The findings explain why.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.734
In the April report, Patrick tested
twenty-eight links.

00:01:04.734 --> 00:01:08.747
Of twelve clearly phishing links, eleven
received the high-risk verdict.

00:01:08.987 --> 00:01:13.272
A convincing phishing page can still
have an ordinary-looking address.

00:01:13.272 --> 00:01:16.863
A low score only tells you the rules
found few warning signs

00:01:16.863 --> 00:01:18.032
in the address itself.
